## §18. POLICY DRIFT DETECTION

### 18.1 Core principle

**Failures that involve governance semantics are NOT generic assertion mismatches — they are policy drift and MUST be classified and escalated before repair.**

### 18.2 Policy drift classification trigger

A failure MUST be classified as `policy_regression_fix` (§14.8) — not generic `production_bug_fix` — when the failing assertion involves ANY of:

| Domain | Examples |
|---|---|
| Fail-closed vs fail-open behavior | `run_pipeline` returning success on invalid input |
| Approval / rejection logic | Human review not triggered when required |
| Confidence thresholds | Routing below threshold allowed through |
| Manual review routing | Auto-approval path invoked when manual review required |
| Replay enforcement | Replayed input accepted without rejection |
| Side-effect guards | Write executed before precondition check |
| Mutation gate bypass | Durable write bypassing UWG |

### 18.3 Policy drift evidence requirement

When a failure is classified as policy drift, evidence MUST include a `## POLICY_DRIFT` section:

```markdown
## POLICY_DRIFT

### Failing test
- nodeid: <exact nodeid>
- exception: <type and message>

### Policy domain
- Domain: <from table in §18.2>
- Intended behavior: <what the policy says SHOULD happen>
- Observed behavior: <what the code actually does>

### Winning contract
- The intended policy is: <exact rule reference, e.g. §10.1, §1.8>
- Resolution layer: <L0–L6 layer where the fix must land>

### Repair plan
- repair_class: policy_regression_fix
- Target file: <production file, not the test file>
- Change required: <minimal description>
```

### 18.4 Forbidden policy drift responses

```
❌ Patching assertion to match broken behavior
❌ Adjusting threshold constant to make test pass without reviewing policy
❌ Adding skipif to avoid policy test
❌ Classifying as production_bug_fix when the bug is in governance semantics
```

### 18.5 CI enforcement

CI MUST detect policy drift commits that are misclassified as `production_bug_fix` when the changed files include governance/routing/protocol modules. `ops_scripts/ci/check_policy_drift_classification.py` MUST flag commits with `repair_class: production_bug_fix` that touch `L0_routing`, `L3_orchestration`, or `L5_safety` modules without a corresponding `## POLICY_DRIFT` section in the linked evidence file.

