## §20. ENVIRONMENT PARITY

### 20.1 Core principle

**Local environment behavior is not valid CI behavior until an environment contract artifact proves parity.**

### 20.2 Environment contract trigger

An environment contract artifact MUST be produced before repair begins when a failure involves ANY of:

| Environment dependency | Examples |
|---|---|
| Subprocess execution | `subprocess.run`, `Popen`, `check_output` |
| WSL / OS boundary | Path separators, symlinks, case sensitivity |
| CUDA / GPU | Model loading, device selection, memory allocation |
| Hugging Face / vLLM | Model weight paths, tokenizer paths, quantization config |
| Import path | `sys.path` manipulation, `PYTHONPATH`, relative imports |
| Filesystem layout | Hardcoded absolute paths, `__file__` assumptions |
| Network / external service | Any call to an external endpoint |

### 20.3 Environment contract artifact format

```markdown
## ENVIRONMENT_CONTRACT

### Failure context
- nodeid: <failing test>
- Environment dependency: <from table in §20.2>

### Local environment state
- Python version: <x.y.z>
- OS: <name and version>
- Relevant env vars: <KEY=value, ...>
- Relevant paths: <path values>

### CI environment expectation
- CI runner: <ubuntu-latest / etc.>
- Expected Python version: <x.y.z>
- Expected env vars: <KEY=value, ...>
- Parity gap: <what differs between local and CI>

### Repair classification
- repair_class: environment_contract_fix
- Parity fix: <what change makes local and CI behave identically>
- Validation: <how to confirm parity after fix>
```

### 20.4 Forbidden environment assumptions

```
❌ Assuming local subprocess path is valid in CI without checking
❌ Hardcoding absolute paths that differ between local and CI
❌ Using sys.path hacks that work locally but fail in CI
❌ Assuming CUDA/vLLM is available in CI without explicit check
❌ Assuming Hugging Face model weights are cached in CI
❌ Fixing locally and declaring convergence before CI validation
```

### 20.5 CI enforcement

CI MUST run `ops_scripts/ci/check_environment_contract.py` which scans for:
- Hardcoded absolute paths outside approved roots
- `subprocess.run` calls without explicit `cwd` or path validation
- `sys.path.insert` / `sys.path.append` in production code
- Imports of `vllm`, `torch`, `cuda` without availability guards

